# Compatibility Directory Route Trial Proposal

This page was not originally intended to define a long-term interface for ElectronNET API · Compatibility Catalog. It began with a very small debate: should `BrowserView` continue handling the existing path, or should `host hook` be split into a separate boundary. Early records described the latter as "current preference," based only on the fact that the failure exits in two pieces of material appeared different. The callers of `native image` were not verified, nor was the rollback scope. The judgment in that version can be retained, but it must not be read backward as a design that has already been approved.

The trial draft added later limited this comparison to the same directory project. Both sides retain the same users, inputs, and observation window; the only change is the route. The original input, version, and first visible result are stored separately; the proposal author organizes the records, and actual users provide feedback. The window is set to two weeks to give the comparison a stopping point, not to forecast the release cadence.

The draft has several exit criteria, and they cannot substitute for one another:

- A single green result cannot show that the route improved;
- Without actual users participating, an internal demo cannot be treated as a result;
- Changing only the display label cannot show that the invocation boundary changed;
- If `socket bridge` cannot be restored on exit, the trial must not continue;
- If issue-localization speed has not improved, or the number of misclassifications has not decreased, end the trial and return to the original entry point.

"Return to the original entry point" was understated in the old draft. It must at least first verify whether the exit action and the rollback entry point use the same recovery logic; the entire directory state cannot be marked complete simply because one local part has been restored. The follow-up items left by ENBB-013 were precisely to verify these two things separately. There is still no evidence here that they have been fully checked.

The trial also does not cover every adjacent object. `WebContents`,`context isolation`,`lifecycle`,`package metadata`,`publish RID`,`session cookies`,`process-global registry` and `native image` all appeared in the field for this route, but they do not form an accepted combination matrix. They represent different compatibility concerns: some are close to page objects, some are close to processes or release materials, and some concern sessions and resources. The proposal did not have enough results at the time to reduce these concerns to one common conclusion.

Several records from 2026 year 1 month made the term "side-by-side comparison" unreliable. On 1 month 5 day, ENBB-002 recorded the same on-duty person taking different actions at expiration on the two sides; this shows that action records need to be reviewed together with the inputs, but does not say which side was correct. On 1 month 9 day, ENBB-007 explicitly pointed out that `original input` and `current result` were not from the same round. On 1 month 15 day, the original failure had not yet been obtained, and the attachments contained only screenshots, so the item was blocked; afterward, the inputs and results for two rounds were split apart, and the missing round was not filled in using another round. On 1 month 19 day, ENBB-002 was blocked again because the on-call teammate for the ElectronNET API · Compatibility Catalog core module was unavailable. This timeline supports only "the evidence window was interrupted," not "a route has already failed" or "a route has already won."

An old discussion once treated `Electron.NET` as a candidate comparator and treated "the same users, inputs, and observation window" as a fair condition. This passage remains because it explains why the proposal did not directly modify the existing entry point; but it provides neither logs showing that the candidate had run nor user feedback. The candidate name cannot be written as an adoption result, and a window with no results cannot be written as a success.

A requirement involving the opposite input also appeared during review. It brought no new conclusion, only an incomplete validation window: when tracing back from `docs/adj-electronnet-api/proposals/rejected-ElectronNET-API-compatibility-catalog-approach.md`, only the local objects actually seen at the time can be filled in; unseen portions remain unknown. DOC-33 can serve as an adjacent directory entry, but it cannot replace this page's record of the route that was not adopted.

From the compatibility directory perspective, the tradeoffs between the candidate options can be documented as follows. The benefit of continuing along `BrowserView` is that the old entry point need not be moved immediately, and existing calling habits need not change concurrently during the trial; the cost is that the failure exit may still be mixed up with issues involving `host hook`. The benefit of splitting out `host hook` is that the two exits can be observed separately; the cost is that rollback is no longer just removing a display label; it also requires confirming that the old entry point, exit action, and adjacent state have not been partially restored. No evidence shows that either cost has actually been incurred, so this page does not write "split apart" as the default plan.

`context isolation` and `session cookies` also cannot be treated as the same switch. The former appeared in discussions of the page boundary, and the latter appeared in the session-state context; if both change at once, a failure will not reveal whether it was caused by the boundary or the state. `process-global registry` is a later-stage runtime concern and cannot be covered by a single page-level green result. The old draft did not leave separate inputs, versions, and results for these objects, so it still lists them only as materials to verify separately, without claiming that combined validation has been completed.

The rollback points therefore remain very specific:

1. The original entry point remains accessible, and the exit action has not been cut off by the new route;
2. The recovery path for `socket bridge` is actually usable, not merely shown in a screenshot;
3. The original input and current result from the same round can be compared side by side;
4. When a failure with an unclear boundary occurs, stop at the current interface first; do not attribute the failure to unverified adjacent objects.

Version 2 once left the long-term owner blank, and that blank field did not disappear just because several review records were added. Version 3 also did not determine the current callers of `native image`, the ownership of `WebContents`, or how release materials should be tied to the route. The version-sync lag of ENBB-007 can still serve only as a look-back entry point; it cannot be expanded into a claim that the directory has already been synchronized. The rollback inconsistency of ENBB-013 can likewise only be retained as a checkpoint.

If this route is brought out for another trial in the future, first add the original failure and the corresponding round, then compare the expiration actions on both sides. Do not use screenshots in place of the original failure, do not use another round's `current result` to fill in the missing `original input`, and do not write a local rollback as global recovery. If these prerequisites still are not met, this page continues to serve as a record of an unadopted proposal, not as an active rule for the compatibility directory.
